  "findings": [
    "Both models produced valid flat schema JSON for the invoice and narrative incident report.",
    "The invoice schema contained 12 fields from each model; the local verifier grounded 10/12 MiniMax and 11/12 Luna values. Quantity/unit-price quotes that contained two numbers were deliberately sent to review.",
    "Luna produced valid incident values and the local verifier grounded 14/15. The boolean power_isolated value was correctly inferred from the sentence but the literal-token verifier sent it to review.",
    "MiniMax proposed 17 incident fields but its value response reached finish_reason=length at 1000 completion tokens; the response was unusable under the production parser.",
    "Across four calls each, MiniMax used 4666 reported tokens and 107704.8 ms summed request time; Luna used 2861 tokens and 15976.1 ms. This tiny sequential sample does not prove general latency or USD-cost superiority.",
    "Neither response usage nor model catalog exposed cost metadata. No USD cost comparison is possible until ICORE rates or settled per-request charges are available."
  ],
  "decision": "For a limited follow-up on synthetic text, prefer Luna 6 for schema and value calls because both documents returned usable JSON within the 1000-token cap and had lower measured request time and token counts here. Keep human approval of dynamic schemas. Do not send private or regulated documents until retention/training terms and per-model prices are confirmed.",
  "limitations": [
    "Two fictional single-page documents, one response per model per stage; no statistical accuracy estimate.",
    "No image, OCR, embeddings, PDF, or image-generation capability was exercised.",
    "No response_format/json_schema parameter was tested; MiniMax strict-schema capability is catalog metadata only.",
    "Provider token usage is reported but actual settled charges are not known.",
    "No official public ICORE pricing schedule or data-processing terms were found in the available sources."
  ],
